✔ a real I/O error while flushing the backup file fails the backup: nothing published, no partial, no sensitive bytes (1515.6334ms)
✔ a file system that cannot fsync is tolerated and reported as a platform limit, not as a failure (1795.5536ms)
✔ a real I/O error flushing the directory after publication keeps the verified backup but reports it as not durable, and old backups are not pruned (3603.4109ms)
✔ a platform that cannot flush a directory is reported as a limit and does not block retention (3065.6717ms)
✔ the real flushes: a platform that can flush a directory does so; Windows reports the documented limit (2613.1224ms)
✔ NATIVE hard-link publication: when the partial's name cannot be removed after the final name exists, the backup is reported as published with the leftover named, not as failed; the next backup sweeps it (3432.1314ms)
﹣ REAL fallback: where this environment refuses hard links, publication uses rename, the partial name is gone, nothing is left over, and the unlink step never runs (1.1345ms) # hard links work here, so the native branch is what runs (see the native test)
✔ REACTION (injected link refusal, any platform): a refused hard link falls back to rename and the backup is complete with no leftover (2591.4986ms)
✔ REACTION (injected; a copy stands in for the link, so this is NOT hard-link or file-system proof): link succeeds, removing the partial name fails -> published with the leftover named, durable, next backup sweeps it; a taken name moves on to -1 (2912.7321ms)
✔ rate-limit rows (client addresses) never reach a completed backup; the table is kept, empty (2150.0351ms)
